requirement:
[p76]       1.2.     ORG-002 Workspace Data Isolation
[p76] 
[p76] 
[p76] Each organisation's:
[p76] 
[p76]    ● users;
[p76]    ● pricing data;
[p76]    ● initiatives;
[p76]    ● proposals;
[p76]    ● analysis;
[p76]    ● comments;
[p76]    ● Approval records and Policy Authorisation records;
[p76]    ● execution records;
[p76]    ● configuration;
[p76]    ● history
[p76] 
[p76] must remain isolated from other organisations.
[p76] 
[p77] A user must never gain access to another organisation's information without explicit
[p77] authorised membership.
[p77] 
[p77]

[p78]       1.6.     ORG-006 Scope-Based Access
[p78] 
[p78] 
[p78] Where relevant, access must be capable of being limited to organisational scope such as:
[p78] 
[p78]    ● category;
[p78]    ● product group;
[p78]    ● region;
[p78]    ● location;
[p78]    ● channel;
[p78]    ● business unit;
[p78]    ● vendor or marketplace scope.
[p78] 
[p78] A user's effective access must be determined by both their assigned role and applicable
[p78] scope.
[p78] 
[p78]

[p78]       1.8.     ORG-008 Workspace Administration
[p78] 
[p78] 
[p78] Authorised administrators must be able to manage:
[p78] 
[p78]    ● organisation profile;
[p78]    ● members;
[p78]    ● roles;
[p78]    ● access scopes;
[p78]    ● workspace configuration;
[p78] 
[p79]    ● pricing policies;
[p79]    ● guardrails;
[p79]    ● workflow configuration.
[p79] 
[p79] Administrative authority must remain separate from commercial pricing authority unless the
[p79] same user has explicitly been granted both.
[p79] 
[p79]

[p80]     1.12.       ORG-012 Administrative Auditability
[p80] 
[p80] 
[p80] Consequential workspace administration actions must be recorded in the audit history.
[p80] 
[p80] This includes:
[p80] 
[p80]    ● user invitations;
[p80]    ● activation and deactivation;
[p80]    ● role changes;
[p80]    ● scope changes;
[p80]    ● sensitive-data access changes;
[p80]    ● ownership changes;
[p80]    ● pricing-policy changes;
[p80]    ● workflow configuration changes;
[p80]    ● guardrail changes.
[p80] 
[p80] Each record must identify:
[p80] 
[p80]    ● action;
[p80]    ● actor;
[p80]    ● timestamp;
[p80]    ● previous state where relevant;
[p80]    ● resulting state.
[p80]

[p81]     1.14.     ORG-014 Minimum Workspace Readiness
[p81] 
[p81] 
[p81] An organisation is ready to begin real pricing work when it has:
[p81] 
[p81]    ● an active organisation workspace;
[p81]    ● at least one authorised workspace administrator;
[p81]    ● at least one active pricing user;
[p81]    ● sufficient product or pricing context to create an initiative;
[p81]    ● applicable pricing controls configured where required;
[p81]    ● an available workflow for progressing the initiative.
[p81] 
[p81] Workspace readiness is distinct from MVP activation. Activation occurs only when the
[p81] organisation completes its first real structured pricing workflow.
[p81]

[p84]         2.2.      DATA-002 Organisation Pricing Dimensions
[p84] 
[p84] 
[p84] The organisation must be able to structure pricing context using relevant dimensions such
[p84] as:
[p84] 
[p84]       ● location;
[p84]       ● region;
[p84]       ● channel;
[p84]       ● customer group;
[p84]       ● business unit;
[p84]       ● vendor;
[p84]       ● price list.
[p84] 
[p84] Dimensions must be reusable across Pricing Initiatives rather than manually recreated for
[p84] every workflow.
[p84] 
[p84]

[p95]     2.19.     DATA-019 Pricing Context Search and Filtering
[p95] 
[p95] 
[p95] Users must be able to locate relevant priceable entities using available identifiers and
[p95] dimensions.
[p95] 
[p95] The MVP should support filtering by relevant attributes such as:
[p95] 
[p96]    ● name;
[p96]    ● SKU or identifier;
[p96]    ● category;
[p96]    ● status;
[p96]    ● location;
[p96]    ● channel.
[p96]

[p100]       3.2.     INIT-002 Initiative Types
[p100] 
[p100] 
[p100] The MVP must support common pricing-work types including:
[p100] 
[p100]    ● price increase;
[p100]    ● price decrease;
[p100]    ● scheduled price review;
[p100]    ● cost-driven repricing;
[p100]    ● margin protection;
[p100]    ● competitor-response pricing;
[p100]    ● promotion or campaign pricing;
[p100]    ● new product or service pricing;
[p100]    ● inventory markdown or clearance;
[p100]    ● category or portfolio review;
[p100] 
[p101]     ● pricing strategy or rule change;
[p101]     ● other organisation-defined pricing work.
[p101] 
[p101] Initiative type should provide structure without forcing every initiative into a rigid workflow.
[p101] 
[p101]

[p101]        3.3.    INIT-003 Pricing Objective
[p101] 
[p101] 
[p101] Every Pricing Initiative must contain a clearly defined pricing objective.
[p101] 
[p101] The objective should explain what the organisation is attempting to achieve, such as:
[p101] 
[p101]     ● protect margin;
[p101]     ● improve profitability;
[p101]     ● increase revenue;
[p101]     ● improve competitiveness;
[p101]     ● support volume growth;
[p101]     ● respond to cost changes;
[p101]     ● clear inventory;
[p101]     ● support market entry;
[p101]     ● execute a promotion;
[p101]     ● establish pricing for a new offering.
[p101] 
[p101] Users must also be able to provide a free-text objective where predefined categories are
[p101] insufficient.
[p101] 
[p101]

[p102]       3.6.      INIT-006 Collaborators
[p102] 
[p102] 
[p102] Authorised users must be able to add relevant participants to an initiative.
[p102] 
[p102] Participants may contribute according to their permissions through activities such as:
[p102] 
[p102]    ● analysis;
[p102]    ● comments;
[p102]    ● supporting information;
[p102]    ● review;
[p102]    ● approval;
[p102]    ● implementation;
[p102]    ● monitoring.
[p102] 
[p102] Participation in an initiative must not override the user's organisation-level permissions.
[p102]

[p103]          3.7.   INIT-007 Initiative Summary
[p103] 
[p103] 
[p103] Every Pricing Initiative must provide a persistent summary containing at minimum:
[p103] 
[p103]     ● title;
[p103]     ● objective;
[p103]     ● initiative type;
[p103]     ● owner;
[p103]     ● pricing scope;
[p103]     ● lifecycle status;
[p103]     ● urgency;
[p103]     ● current proposal state where applicable;
[p103]     ● responsible next participant or action where applicable.
[p103] 
[p103] This summary must remain understandable without requiring the user to reconstruct the
[p103] initiative from its activity history.
[p103] 
[p103]

[p103]          3.8.   INIT-008 Lifecycle State
[p103] 
[p103] 
[p103] A Pricing Initiative must support the following primary lifecycle states:
[p103] 
[p103]     ● Initiated;
[p103]     ● In Development;
[p103]     ● In Review;
[p103]     ● Awaiting Approval;
[p103]     ● Ready for Execution;
[p103]     ● Implemented;
[p103]     ● Monitoring;
[p103]     ● Completed;
[p103]     ● Cancelled.
[p103] 
[p103] These states represent the overall progression of the Pricing Initiative.
[p103] 
[p103] Proposal-specific decision states such as Approved, Rejected, Changes Requested, or
[p103] Authorised Within Policy must not be represented as Pricing Initiative lifecycle states.
[p103] 
[p103] The system must clearly display the current Pricing Initiative lifecycle state to authorised
[p103] users.
[p103]

[p108]      3.16.      INIT-016 Initiative Search and Filtering
[p108] 
[p108] 
[p108] Users must be able to locate Pricing Initiatives using relevant criteria such as:
[p108] 
[p108]     ● title;
[p108]     ● owner;
[p108]     ● status;
[p108]     ● initiative type;
[p108]     ● urgency;
[p108]     ● product or category;
[p108]     ● location or channel;
[p108]     ● creation date;
[p108]     ● effective date.
[p108] 
[p108] Search results must respect the user's access scope.
[p108] 
[p108]

[p112]       4.2.    BUILD-002 Pricing Action Definition
[p112] 
[p112] 
[p112] Users must be able to define the pricing action they are constructing.
[p112] 
[p112] Supported actions should include:
[p112] 
[p112]    ● set a new price;
[p112]    ● increase price;
[p112]    ● decrease price;
[p112]    ● apply a percentage adjustment;
[p112]    ● apply an absolute-value adjustment;
[p112] 
[p113]     ● maintain or protect a target margin;
[p113]     ● apply a markup;
[p113]     ● establish promotional pricing;
[p113]     ● markdown pricing;
[p113]     ● create pricing for a new product or service;
[p113]     ● define or modify a pricing rule;
[p113]     ● define a custom pricing action using supported builder components.
[p113] 
[p113] Quantitative pricing actions covered by ANALYSIS-002 must use the deterministic
[p113] calculation definitions established in Section 3.5 where the required inputs are available.
[p113] 
[p113] New-product or service pricing, pricing rules and custom pricing actions may contain
[p113] structured elements that are not themselves deterministically calculable.
[p113] 
[p113] The Builder must not represent a pricing outcome as deterministically calculated unless the
[p113] required inputs, operators and calculation method are supported by the analytical calculation
[p113] layer.
[p113] 
[p113] Where part of a pricing action depends on qualitative judgement, unsupported logic or a
[p113] manually determined commercial value, that component must remain distinguishable from
[p113] system-calculated quantitative output.
[p113] 
[p113]

[p122]     4.19.     BUILD-019 AI-Assisted Construction
[p122] 
[p122] 
[p122] Users must be able to invoke AI assistance while constructing pricing work.
[p122] 
[p122] AI may help:
[p122] 
[p122]    ● translate business intent into structured pricing logic;
[p122] 
[p123]    ● draft pricing rules;
[p123]    ● explain existing logic;
[p123]    ● identify missing information;
[p123]    ● identify contradictory conditions;
[p123]    ● refine assumptions;
[p123]    ● propose alternative approaches;
[p123]    ● explain the likely effect of changes.
[p123] 
[p123] AI-generated changes must remain proposals until explicitly applied by an authorised human
[p123] user.
[p123] 
[p123]

[p125]     4.24.    BUILD-024 Builder Output
[p125] 
[p125] 
[p125] The final output of the Pricing Builder is structured pricing work that can be consumed
[p125] consistently by subsequent MVP capabilities.
[p125] 
[p125] Where applicable, the Builder output must preserve:
[p125] 
[p125]    ● pricing action;
[p125]    ● affected pricing scope;
[p125]    ● relevant pricing baseline;
[p125]    ● structured pricing logic;
[p125]    ● calculation inputs;
[p125]    ● explicit assumptions;
[p125]    ● material parameters;
[p125]    ● conditions;
[p125]    ● constraints;
[p125]    ● references to applicable organisational Guardrails and, where available, their
[p125]        authoritative ANALYSIS-014 evaluation results;
[p125]    ● exceptions;
[p125]    ● deterministic calculation status;
[p125]    ● applicable calculation method;
[p125]    ● resulting proposed pricing output;
[p125]    ● distinction between system-calculated values and manually determined or qualitative
[p125]        components.
[p125] 
[p126] Organisational Guardrail references contained in Builder output must point to the applicable
[p126] governance-defined Guardrail rather than represent a separately authored Builder Guardrail.
[p126] 
[p126] Where an ANALYSIS-014 evaluation is not yet available, the Builder output may preserve
[p126] the applicable Guardrail reference without inventing an evaluation result.
[p126] 
[p126] For deterministically calculable pricing work, the Builder output must contain sufficient
[p126] information for the analytical layer to reproduce the result using the calculation definitions in
[p126] Section 3.5.
[p126] 
[p126] For pricing work that contains non-deterministic components, the output must preserve those
[p126] components without falsely converting them into calculated quantitative results.
[p126] 
[p126] The Builder output must provide sufficient information for:
[p126] 
[p126]    ● pricing analysis;
[p126]    ● Scenario evaluation;
[p126]    ● collaboration;
[p126]    ● review;
[p126]    ● Pricing Proposal creation;
[p126]    ● organisational decision;
[p126]    ● execution preparation;
[p126]    ● historical understanding.
[p126] 
[p126] The Pricing Builder therefore serves as the controlled bridge between a business pricing
[p126] objective and the specific pricing action ultimately submitted for organisational decision.
[p126]

[p128]       5.1.    ANALYSIS-001 Initiative-Level Analysis
[p128] 
[p128] 
[p128] Every Pricing Initiative must provide an analysis area connected to:
[p128] 
[p128]     ● the affected pricing scope;
[p128]     ● current pricing data;
[p128]     ● relevant cost records and the applicable unit cost selected or assumed for the
[p128]         analysis;
[p128]     ● pricing logic;
[p128]     ● assumptions;
[p128]     ● scenarios;
[p128]     ● applicable guardrails.
[p128] 
[p128] Analysis must remain part of the Pricing Initiative rather than exist as a disconnected
[p128] calculator.
[p128] 
[p128]

[p128]       5.2.    ANALYSIS-002 Deterministic Pricing Calculation Coverage
[p128] 
[p128] 
[p128] Where the required inputs are available, the MVP must provide deterministic calculation
[p128] support for the quantitative pricing actions defined in the Pricing Builder.
[p128] 
[p128] The analytical calculation layer must support, where applicable:
[p128] 
[p128]     ● direct entry of a proposed selling price;
[p128]     ● percentage increase or decrease from a defined baseline price;
[p128]     ● absolute-value increase or decrease from a defined baseline price;
[p128]     ● derivation of a selling price from a target gross-margin percentage;
[p128]     ● derivation of a selling price from a target markup percentage;
[p128]     ● promotional or discount pricing expressed as a percentage reduction;
[p128] 
[p129]    ● promotional or discount pricing expressed as an absolute-value reduction;
[p129]    ● markdown pricing expressed through a supported percentage or absolute-value
[p129]          adjustment;
[p129]    ● calculation of the resulting selling price from supported structured pricing logic where
[p129]          all required inputs and operators are deterministically evaluable.
[p129] 
[p129] For the current and resulting proposed pricing position, the MVP must calculate or preserve,
[p129] where applicable:
[p129] 
[p129]    ● current selling price;
[p129]    ● proposed selling price;
[p129]    ● absolute price change;
[p129]    ● percentage price change;
[p129]    ● applicable unit cost;
[p129]    ● gross profit per unit;
[p129]    ● gross margin percentage;
[p129]    ● markup percentage.
[p129] 
[p129] A pricing action must not be presented as deterministically calculated where the system
[p129] does not have sufficient inputs or a supported calculation method to derive its result.
[p129] 
[p129] Pricing approaches that contain qualitative judgement, unsupported custom logic or
[p129] conditions that cannot be deterministically evaluated may still be represented within the
[p129] Pricing Initiative, but the system must distinguish those elements from calculated quantitative
[p129] outputs.
[p129] 
[p129] The same calculation definitions must be used consistently across the Pricing Builder
[p129] preview, pricing analysis, Scenarios, Proposal preparation and guardrail evaluation.
[p129] 
[p129] AI must not independently define or alter these deterministic calculation methods.
[p129] 
[p129]

[p135]       5.8.    ANALYSIS-008 Scenario Creation
[p135] 
[p135] 
[p135] Authorised users must be able to create multiple pricing scenarios within an initiative.
[p135] 
[p135] A scenario represents an alternative pricing approach under consideration.
[p135] 
[p135] Each scenario should contain, where relevant:
[p135] 
[p135]    ● name;
[p135]    ● pricing action;
[p135]    ● proposed price or pricing logic;
[p135]    ● assumptions;
[p135]    ● affected scope;
[p135]    ● calculated outputs;
[p135]    ● guardrail status;
[p135]    ● creator;
[p135]    ● timestamp.
[p135]

[p135]     5.10.     ANALYSIS-010 Scenario Comparison
[p135] 
[p135] 
[p135] Users must be able to compare scenarios using relevant measures.
[p135] 
[p135] Where inputs are available, comparison should include:
[p135] 
[p136]    ● proposed price;
[p136]    ● price change;
[p136]    ● gross profit;
[p136]    ● gross margin;
[p136]    ● markup;
[p136]    ● applicable guardrail status;
[p136]    ● material assumptions.
[p136] 
[p136] Scenario comparison must make trade-offs visible rather than presenting only a
[p136] recommended option.
[p136] 
[p136]

[p140]     5.19.     ANALYSIS-019 Qualitative Decision Inputs
[p140] 
[p140] 
[p140] The analysis area must be able to reference qualitative decision inputs that materially affect
[p140] interpretation of pricing work but are not deterministic calculation outputs.
[p140] 
[p141] Qualitative decision inputs may originate from:
[p141] 
[p141]    ● structured Pricing Context governed by Section 3.2;
[p141]    ● Evidence governed by Section 3.7;
[p141]    ● explicit Assumptions governed by ANALYSIS-017;
[p141]    ● attributable user-authored analytical interpretation.
[p141] 
[p141] Relevant examples may include:
[p141] 
[p141]    ● competitor observations;
[p141]    ● customer or sales feedback;
[p141]    ● supplier information;
[p141]    ● market conditions;
[p141]    ● operational constraints;
[p141]    ● commercial judgement.
[p141] 
[p141] The analytical layer must preserve the originating information type rather than creating a
[p141] separate competing qualitative-context object.
[p141] 
[p141] Analysis must not:
[p141] 
[p141]    ● represent Evidence as verified structured organisational data;
[p141]    ● represent an Assumption as a verified fact;
[p141]    ● represent user interpretation as Evidence;
[p141]    ● represent AI interpretation as recorded organisational fact;
[p141]    ● convert a qualitative input into a deterministic calculation output merely because it
[p141]        influenced the analysis.
[p141] 
[p141] Where a qualitative decision input materially supports a submitted Pricing Proposal, the
[p141] Proposal and historical record must preserve sufficient reference to its originating
[p141] information type and relevant source or version context.
[p141] 
[p141]

[p141]     5.20.     ANALYSIS-020 AI-Assisted Analysis
[p141] 
[p141] 
[p141] Users must be able to invoke AI to assist in interpreting the available pricing analysis.
[p141] 
[p141] AI may:
[p141] 
[p141]    ● explain calculations;
[p141]    ● summarise differences between scenarios;
[p141] 
[p142]    ● identify material trade-offs;
[p142]    ● identify missing information;
[p142]    ● surface inconsistencies;
[p142]    ● explain guardrail breaches;
[p142]    ● highlight potentially consequential assumptions;
[p142]    ● help users formulate additional scenarios.
[p142] 
[p142] AI-generated interpretation must not alter underlying calculations.
[p142] 
[p142]

[p142]      5.22.    ANALYSIS-022 Calculation Traceability
[p142] 
[p142] 
[p142] Users must be able to understand and reproduce the basis of consequential deterministic
[p142] pricing calculations.
[p142] 
[p142] For each material calculated output, the MVP must preserve enough calculation context to
[p142] determine, where applicable:
[p142] 
[p142]    ● pricing action;
[p142]    ● affected pricing object or scope;
[p142]    ● calculation baseline;
[p142]    ● source input values;
[p142]    ● source of those values;
[p142]    ● explicitly assumed values;
[p142]    ● calculation parameter or adjustment value;
[p142]    ● operator;
[p142]    ● formula or deterministic calculation method used;
[p142]    ● resulting unrounded calculated value;
[p142]    ● explicitly applied rounding or final-value adjustment, where applicable;
[p142]    ● resulting proposed value used by subsequent pricing work.
[p142] 
[p143] A calculated output must remain distinguishable from a directly entered value, explicit
[p143] assumption or manually determined commercial value.
[p143] 
[p143] Calculation traceability must remain consistent across the Pricing Builder, analysis,
[p143] Scenarios, Proposal preparation and guardrail evaluation.
[p143] 
[p143] Pricing analysis must therefore be reproducible from the preserved decision-relevant pricing
[p143] context.
[p143] 
[p143]

[p145]     5.27.     ANALYSIS-027 Analysis Snapshot
[p145] 
[p145] 
[p145] When a Pricing Proposal is submitted, the decision-relevant analysis supporting that
[p145] Proposal version must be preserved.
[p145] 
[p145] Where cost-dependent calculations form part of the analysis, the preserved analysis must
[p145] retain, where applicable:
[p145] 
[p145]    ● the Applicable Unit Cost used;
[p145]    ● cost basis;
[p145]    ● currency;
[p145]    ● applicable unit of measure;
[p145]    ● source Cost Record or explicit cost assumption;
[p145]    ● relevant effective context;
[p145]    ● calculated financial outputs dependent on that cost.
[p145] 
[p145] Subsequent changes to:
[p145] 
[p145]    ● Cost Records;
[p145]    ● selection of the Applicable Unit Cost;
[p145]    ● cost basis;
[p145]    ● current prices;
[p145]    ● assumptions;
[p145]    ● Scenarios;
[p145]    ● underlying workspace data
[p145] 
[p145] must not silently modify, recalculate or reinterpret the analysis associated with an already
[p145] submitted Proposal version.
[p145] 
[p146] Where an explicit assumed Applicable Unit Cost formed part of the submitted analysis, that
[p146] value must remain identifiable as an assumption rather than verified organisational cost
[p146] data.
[p146] 
[p146] Historical analysis must continue to reflect the exact cost context and other decision-relevant
[p146] inputs used when the Proposal version was submitted.
[p146] 
[p146]

[p149]        6.1.     PROP-001 Pricing Proposal Definition
[p149] 
[p149] 
[p149] A Pricing Proposal must represent a specific proposed pricing action associated with one
[p149] Pricing Initiative.
[p149] 
[p149] The Proposal must answer, at minimum:
[p149] 
[p149]     ● what pricing action is being proposed;
[p149]     ● which priceable entities and pricing dimensions are affected;
[p149]     ● what the relevant current or baseline pricing position is;
[p149]     ● what the proposed pricing position or rule is;
[p149]     ● why the action is being proposed;
[p149]     ● what material assumptions support it;
[p149]     ● what analysis supports the action;
[p149]     ● which applicable guardrails were evaluated;
[p149]     ● when the proposed action should become effective.
[p149] 
[p149] A Pricing Proposal must not represent the entire Pricing Initiative.
[p149] 
[p149]

[p149]        6.2.     PROP-002 Initiative Relationship
[p149] 
[p150] Every Pricing Proposal must belong to exactly one Pricing Initiative.
[p150] 
[p150] The Pricing Initiative must preserve the relationship between the Proposal and the
[p150] underlying:
[p150] 
[p150]     ● pricing objective;
[p150]     ● pricing scope;
[p150]     ● pricing context;
[p150]     ● Pricing Builder work;
[p150]     ● pricing logic;
[p150]     ● analysis;
[p150]     ● Scenarios where applicable;
[p150]     ● assumptions;
[p150]     ● evidence;
[p150]     ● collaboration;
[p150]     ● workflow;
[p150]     ● organisational decision records, including approvals and policy authorisations;
[p150]     ● execution records.
[p150] 
[p150] A Pricing Proposal must not exist as disconnected pricing work outside its originating Pricing
[p150] Initiative.
[p150] 
[p150]

[p151]        6.5.     PROP-005 Proposal Identification
[p151] 
[p151] 
[p151] Every Pricing Proposal must have a persistent identifier within its Pricing Initiative.
[p151] 
[p151] Every controlled Proposal version must also have a unique version identifier.
[p151] 
[p151] The system must preserve, at minimum:
[p151] 
[p151]     ● Proposal identifier;
[p151]     ● Proposal version;
[p151]     ● originating Pricing Initiative;
[p151] 
[p152]    ● current or historical status;
[p152]    ● creator;
[p152]    ● creation timestamp;
[p152]    ● submitting user where applicable;
[p152]    ● submission timestamp where applicable.
[p152] 
[p152] Users must be able to distinguish the current Proposal version from previous versions.
[p152] 
[p152]

[p152]       6.6.    PROP-006 Proposal Content
[p152] 
[p152] 
[p152] Before formal submission, the Pricing Proposal must contain sufficient information for an
[p152] authorised reviewer or approver to understand the proposed pricing decision without
[p152] reconstructing it from disconnected parts of the Initiative.
[p152] 
[p152] Where applicable, the Proposal must contain or reference:
[p152] 
[p152]    ● affected pricing scope;
[p152]    ● current or baseline pricing state;
[p152]    ● proposed pricing action;
[p152]    ● proposed prices or pricing logic;
[p152]    ● relevant currency;
[p152]    ● pricing objective;
[p152]    ● supporting rationale;
[p152]    ● material assumptions;
[p152]    ● relevant analysis and calculated outputs;
[p152]    ● applicable guardrail evaluation;
[p152]    ● material exceptions;
[p152]    ● relevant supporting evidence;
[p152]    ● intended effective date or effective period;
[p152]    ● relevant implementation information required for operational handoff.
[p152] 
[p152] Information referenced from another Initiative object must remain bound to the exact version
[p152] used by the Proposal.
[p152] 
[p152]

[p152]       6.7.    PROP-007 Proposal Source Traceability
[p152] 
[p152] 
[p152] The Proposal must preserve traceability to the pricing work from which it was created.
[p152] 
[p152] Where applicable, users must be able to determine:
[p152] 
[p153]    ● which Pricing Builder logic produced the proposed action;
[p153]    ● which Scenario was selected;
[p153]    ● which calculations informed the Proposal;
[p153]    ● which assumptions were used;
[p153]    ● which pricing-context values were relied upon;
[p153]    ● which evidence supported the rationale;
[p153]    ● which guardrails were evaluated.
[p153] 
[p153] The system must not silently replace these sources after the Proposal has been submitted.
[p153] 
[p153]

[p162]     6.22.     PROP-022 Proposal Historical Integrity
[p162] 
[p162] 
[p162] Every consequential Pricing Proposal must remain historically understandable after the
[p162] Pricing Initiative progresses or is completed.
[p162] 
[p162] For each Proposal version, an authorised user should be able to determine:
[p162] 
[p162]    ● what was proposed;
[p162]    ● which Pricing Initiative it belonged to;
[p162]    ● which pricing scope it affected;
[p162]    ● what baseline or current pricing state was used;
[p162]    ● what pricing action was proposed;
[p162]    ● which assumptions and analysis supported it;
[p162]    ● which guardrails applied;
[p162]    ● which evidence supported the decision;
[p162]    ● who submitted it;
[p162]    ● which version was submitted;
[p162] 
[p163]    ● what decision was made;
[p163]    ● who made the decision;
[p163]    ● whether changes were requested or the Proposal was rejected;
[p163]    ● whether the Proposal was Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
[p163]    ● whether the version later became Superseded or Expired;
[p163]    ● whether it became the basis for execution.
[p163] 
[p163] If the exact decision represented by a historical Proposal version cannot be reconstructed
[p163] from Owómidé, the Proposal record is incomplete.
[p163]

[p165]       7.1.     COLLAB-001 Contextual Collaboration
[p165] 
[p165] 
[p165] Users must be able to collaborate within a Pricing Initiative.
[p165] 
[p165] Collaboration may be attached to:
[p165] 
[p165]    ● the overall initiative;
[p165]    ● pricing scope;
[p165]    ● analysis;
[p165]    ● scenarios;
[p165]    ● pricing logic;
[p165]    ● proposals;
[p165]    ● review activity;
[p165]    ● execution or monitoring records.
[p165] 
[p165] Users should be able to understand what object or decision context a contribution refers to.
[p165] 
[p165]

[p166]       7.5.    COLLAB-005 Assignments
[p166] 
[p166] 
[p166] Authorised users must be able to assign specific actions to relevant participants.
[p166] 
[p166] Assignments may include tasks such as:
[p166] 
[p166]    ● provide cost information;
[p166]    ● review assumptions;
[p166]    ● validate commercial input;
[p166]    ● review a scenario;
[p166]    ● provide supporting evidence;
[p166]    ● complete review;
[p166]    ● implement an authorised pricing action;
[p166]    ● record monitoring information.
[p166] 
[p166] Each assignment should contain:
[p166] 
[p166]    ● assigned user;
[p166]    ● action required;
[p166] 
[p167]    ● associated initiative or pricing object;
[p167]    ● status;
[p167]    ● due date where applicable;
[p167]    ● creator;
[p167]    ● creation timestamp.
[p167]

[p167]       7.7.     COLLAB-007 Supporting Evidence
[p167] 
[p167] 
[p167] Users must be able to attach Evidence supporting or challenging pricing work.
[p167] 
[p167] Evidence is supporting material, observation or externally supplied information used to
[p167] inform, substantiate or question pricing work. Evidence does not become authoritative
[p167] structured Pricing Context merely because it is attached to a Pricing Initiative, analysis,
[p167] Scenario or Pricing Proposal.
[p167] 
[p167] Supported Evidence may include:
[p167] 
[p167]    ● uploaded documents;
[p167]    ● spreadsheets or extracts;
[p167]    ● images;
[p167]    ● internal reports;
[p167]    ● external links;
[p167]    ● manually entered observations;
[p167]    ● supplier information;
[p167]    ● competitor observations;
[p167]    ● customer or sales feedback;
[p167]    ● other relevant supporting material.
[p167] 
[p167] Evidence must remain attached or referenced to the pricing work it supports.
[p167] 
[p168] Evidence may support:
[p168] 
[p168]     ● an Assumption;
[p168]     ● business rationale;
[p168]     ● analytical interpretation;
[p168]     ● a proposed pricing action;
[p168]     ● a review or organisational decision;
[p168]     ● another supported decision-relevant element.
[p168] 
[p168] Evidence must remain distinguishable from:
[p168] 
[p168]     ● structured Pricing Context governed by Section 3.2;
[p168]     ● explicit Assumptions;
[p168]     ● deterministic calculations;
[p168]     ● AI-generated interpretation;
[p168]     ● comments or discussion.
[p168] 
[p168] Where information originating from Evidence is intentionally converted into structured
[p168] Owómidé Pricing Context, the resulting structured record must satisfy the applicable Section

[p168]       7.8.       COLLAB-008 Evidence Metadata
[p168] 
[p168] 
[p168] Evidence records should preserve, where available:
[p168] 
[p168]     ● title or description;
[p168]     ● contributor;
[p168]     ● submission date;
[p168]     ● source;
[p168]     ● related pricing object;
[p168]     ● observation date or effective date where relevant.
[p168] 
[p168] Users must be able to distinguish evidence from structured system data and AI-generated
[p168] interpretation.
[p168]

[p169]       7.9.     COLLAB-009 Manual Market Observations
[p169] 
[p169] 
[p169] Because relevant pricing intelligence may originate outside digitised systems, authorised
[p169] users must be able to record manually gathered market observations.
[p169] 
[p169] An observation should support fields such as:
[p169] 
[p169]     ● observation type;
[p169]     ● subject or competitor;
[p169]     ● observed value or price where applicable;
[p169]     ● location;
[p169]     ● channel;
[p169]     ● observation date;
[p169]     ● source or method;
[p169]     ● notes;
[p169]     ● submitting user.
[p169] 
[p169] Such observations must not be represented as automatically verified or real-time
[p169] intelligence.
[p169] 
[p169]

[p169]      7.10.     COLLAB-010 Evidence Relevance
[p169] 
[p169] 
[p169] Users should be able to associate evidence with the specific part of pricing work it informs.
[p169] 
[p169] For example, evidence may support:
[p169] 
[p169]     ● an assumption;
[p169]     ● competitor comparison;
[p169]     ● proposed price;
[p169]     ● cost change;
[p169]     ● commercial rationale;
[p169]     ● scenario;
[p169]     ● organisational decision.
[p169] 
[p169] This prevents the initiative from becoming an unstructured attachment repository.
[p169] 
[p169]

[p170]     7.13.       COLLAB-013 Collaboration Activity
[p170] 
[p170] 
[p170] Relevant collaboration events must appear in the Pricing Initiative activity history.
[p170] 
[p170] This includes:
[p170] 
[p170]    ● comments;
[p170]    ● replies;
[p170]    ● mentions;
[p170]    ● assignments;
[p170]    ● assignment completion;
[p170]    ● evidence additions;
[p170]    ● review feedback;
[p170] 
[p171]    ● issue resolution.
[p171]

[p175]       8.1.     FLOW-001 Bounded Workflow Configuration
[p175] 
[p175] 
[p175] Authorised administrators must be able to configure supported pricing-specific workflow
[p175] behaviour through a bounded Workflow Configuration Profile.
[p175] 
[p175] A Workflow Configuration Profile may define supported parameters including:
[p175] 
[p175]    ● whether formal review requirements apply within the canonical In Review stage;
[p175]    ● required reviewer roles or participants;
[p175]    ● applicable organisational decision path;
[p175]    ● item-level approval requirements where applicable;
[p175]    ● required approver roles;
[p175]    ● supported sequential or multiple-approval arrangements;
[p175]    ● supported approval-routing conditions;
[p175]    ● target response dates or approval deadlines;
[p175]    ● supported escalation behaviour;
[p175]    ● authorised delegates where organisational policy permits.
[p175] 
[p175] Where policy authorisation is enabled, the Workflow Configuration Profile may determine
[p175] whether eligible pricing work is routed into the policy-authorisation path rather than item-level
[p175] approval.
[p175] 
[p176] Workflow configuration must not independently define policy-authorisation eligibility,
[p176] authority, qualification thresholds or policy conditions.
[p176] 
[p176] Those requirements remain governed by FLOW-022, FLOW-023, GOV-027 and GOV-028.
[p176] 
[p176] Workflow Configuration Profiles must operate within the canonical Pricing Initiative lifecycle
[p176] defined in Section 2.2.
[p176] 
[p176] Administrators must not be able to use workflow configuration to:
[p176] 
[p176]     ● create new Pricing Initiative lifecycle states;
[p176]     ● redefine the meaning of canonical lifecycle states;
[p176]     ● create arbitrary state-transition graphs;
[p176]     ● redefine Pricing Proposal status or validity;
[p176]     ● create unrestricted custom workflow stages;
[p176]     ● execute user-authored scripts or code;
[p176]     ● introduce arbitrary executable expressions;
[p176]     ● create arbitrary user-defined triggers or action chains;
[p176]     ● perform general-purpose organisational automation;
[p176]     ● bypass Proposal readiness requirements;
[p176]     ● bypass approval authority;
[p176]     ● bypass maker-checker requirements;
[p176]     ● bypass Guardrails or other Governance controls.
[p176] 
[p176] Routing and escalation conditions must use supported pricing-specific condition types
[p176] exposed by the MVP.
[p176] 
[p176] A Workflow Configuration Profile therefore configures supported behaviour inside the
[p176] Owómidé pricing lifecycle rather than defining a new workflow engine.
[p176] 
[p176]

[p193]      8.31.      FLOW-031 Workflow Visibility
[p193] 
[p193] 
[p193] Relevant users must be able to understand:
[p193] 
[p193]     ● current workflow state;
[p193]     ● completed stages;
[p193]     ● outstanding stages;
[p193]     ● current responsible party;
[p193]     ● required next action;
[p193]     ● pending approvals;
[p193]     ● blocking issues.
[p193] 
[p193] The workflow state must not depend on users reconstructing status from comments or
[p193] activity logs.
[p193] 
[p193]

[p193]      8.32.      FLOW-032 Workflow Notifications
[p193] 
[p193] 
[p193] Relevant workflow events must trigger notifications where appropriate, including:
[p193] 
[p193]     ● review request;
[p193]     ● approval request;
[p193]     ● changes requested;
[p193]     ● rejection;
[p193]     ● approval;
[p193]     ● policy authorisation;
[p193]     ● escalation;
[p193]     ● execution readiness.
[p193] 
[p193] Notifications must link users back to the relevant Pricing Initiative and proposal context.
[p193] 
[p193]

[p193]      8.33.      FLOW-033 Workflow Audit History
[p193] 
[p193] 
[p193] Every consequential workflow event must be preserved in chronological history.
[p193] 
[p193] This includes:
[p193] 
[p193]     ● submission;
[p193] 
[p194]    ● routing;
[p194]    ● review completion;
[p194]    ● approval;
[p194]    ● rejection;
[p194]    ● changes requested;
[p194]    ● escalation;
[p194]    ● delegation;
[p194]    ● expiration;
[p194]    ● re-submission;
[p194]    ● policy authorisation;
[p194]    ● execution readiness.

[p196]      8.36.     FLOW-036 Workflow Integrity Principle
[p196] 
[p196] 
[p196] For every consequential Pricing Proposal decision, the MVP must be able to determine:
[p196] 
[p196]     ● who submitted the Proposal;
[p196]     ● which exact controlled Proposal version was submitted;
[p196]     ● which organisational decision path applied;
[p196]     ● which workflow and governance rules applied;
[p196]     ● who reviewed it where review was required;
[p196]     ● who approved, rejected or requested changes where item-level approval applied;
[p196]     ● which policy and qualification conditions produced Authorised Within Policy where
[p196]         policy authorisation applied;
[p196]     ● under what authority the decision occurred;
[p196]     ● what materially changed after submission or review;
[p196]     ● which exact Proposal version ultimately provided execution authority;
[p196]     ● whether the actual implementation matched that authorised decision baseline.
[p196] 
[p196] Approval and policy authorisation must remain distinguishable historical decision paths.
[p196] 
[p196] If these questions cannot be answered from the system record, the workflow is not
[p196] sufficiently controlled.
[p196]

[p198]       9.2.     GOV-002 Core MVP Roles
[p198] 
[p198] 
[p198] The MVP should support the following core roles:
[p198] 
[p198]     ● Account Owner;
[p198]     ● Workspace Admin;
[p198]     ● Pricing Manager;
[p198]     ● Pricing Analyst;
[p198]     ● Pricing Contributor;
[p198]     ● Finance Approver;
[p198]     ● Commercial Approver;
[p198]     ● Executive Approver;
[p198]     ● Executor;
[p198]     ● Viewer;
[p198]     ● Auditor.
[p198] 
[p198] Additional specialised roles may be configured using supported permission combinations
[p198] where necessary.
[p198]

[p199]       9.4.    GOV-004 Workspace Admin
[p199] 
[p199] 
[p199] A Workspace Admin may manage supported workspace configuration including:
[p199] 
[p199]    ● users;
[p199]    ● roles;
[p199]    ● access scopes;
[p199]    ● workflow configuration;
[p199]    ● pricing policies;
[p199]    ● guardrails;
[p199]    ● organisational settings.
[p199] 
[p199] Administrative authority must remain distinct from commercial pricing authority.
[p199] 
[p199]

[p199]       9.5.    GOV-005 Pricing Manager
[p199] 
[p199] 
[p199] A Pricing Manager may, within authorised scope:
[p199] 
[p199]    ● create and manage Pricing Initiatives;
[p199]    ● construct pricing work;
[p199]    ● manage collaborators;
[p199]    ● review pricing analysis;
[p199]    ● create Pricing Proposals;
[p199]    ● submit proposals;
[p199] 
[p200]    ● coordinate pricing workflows.
[p200] 
[p200] Approval authority must be separately granted where required.
[p200] 
[p200]

[p200]       9.6.    GOV-006 Pricing Analyst
[p200] 
[p200] 
[p200] A Pricing Analyst may, within authorised scope:
[p200] 
[p200]    ● access relevant pricing context;
[p200]    ● perform pricing analysis;
[p200]    ● create scenarios;
[p200]    ● contribute to Pricing Builder work;
[p200]    ● prepare pricing recommendations;
[p200]    ● collaborate on initiatives.
[p200] 
[p200] An analyst must not automatically receive formal approval authority.
[p200] 
[p200]

[p202]     9.13.     GOV-013 Auditor
[p202] 
[p202] 
[p202] An Auditor may inspect relevant historical pricing records, including where authorised:
[p202] 
[p202]    ● proposals;
[p202]    ● versions;
[p202]    ● approvals;
[p202]    ● policy-authorisation records;
[p202]    ● workflow history;
[p202]    ● execution history;
[p202]    ● administrative changes;
[p202]    ● audit records.
[p202] 
[p202] Audit access is read-only unless additional permissions are explicitly granted.
[p202] 
[p202]

[p202]     9.15.     GOV-015 Permission Categories
[p202] 
[p203] The permission system must distinguish actions such as:
[p203] 
[p203]    ● View;
[p203]    ● Create;
[p203]    ● Edit;
[p203]    ● Analyse;
[p203]    ● Comment;
[p203]    ● Assign;
[p203]    ● Review;
[p203]    ● Submit;
[p203]    ● Approve;
[p203]    ● Reject;
[p203]    ● Request Changes;
[p203]    ● Execute;
[p203]    ● Configure;
[p203]    ● Export;
[p203]    ● Audit.
[p203] 
[p203] Permissions must be evaluated against the relevant resource and scope.
[p203] 
[p203]

[p203]     9.16.      GOV-016 Resource-Level Permissions
[p203] 
[p203] 
[p203] Permissions must apply to defined resource types such as:
[p203] 
[p203]    ● organisation settings;
[p203]    ● users;
[p203]    ● pricing data;
[p203]    ● costs;
[p203]    ● Pricing Initiatives;
[p203]    ● Pricing Builder logic;
[p203]    ● scenarios;
[p203]    ● Pricing Proposals;
[p203]    ● approvals;
[p203]    ● policy-authorisation records;
[p203]    ● execution records;
[p203]    ● monitoring information;
[p203]    ● audit history.
[p203] 
[p204] A user may therefore have different authority across different product objects.
[p204] 
[p204]

[p204]      9.17.      GOV-017 Scope-Based Authority
[p204] 
[p204] 
[p204] Permissions must support limiting authority by organisational scope.
[p204] 
[p204] Relevant scopes may include:
[p204] 
[p204]     ● business unit;
[p204]     ● category;
[p204]     ● product group;
[p204]     ● product;
[p204]     ● region;
[p204]     ● location;
[p204]     ● channel;
[p204]     ● customer group;
[p204]     ● vendor;
[p204]     ● price list.
[p204] 
[p204] A user authorised for one scope must not automatically gain authority outside that scope.
[p204] 
[p204]

[p205]      9.21.       GOV-021 Cost Visibility
[p205] 
[p205] 
[p205] Access to sensitive cost information must be independently controllable.
[p205] 
[p205] A user may participate in pricing work without necessarily being permitted to view cost data.
[p205] 
[p205] Cost visibility controls must apply across:
[p205] 
[p205]     ● product records;
[p205]     ● initiative context;
[p205]     ● analysis;
[p205]     ● scenarios;
[p205]     ● proposals;
[p205]     ● exports;
[p205]     ● AI interactions.
[p205]

[p209]      9.26.      GOV-026 Approval Authority Levels
[p209] 
[p210] Where required, the organisation must be able to define approval authority according to
[p210] supported thresholds.
[p210] 
[p210] Authority may depend on factors such as:
[p210] 
[p210]    ● magnitude of price change;
[p210]    ● margin level;
[p210]    ● discount level;
[p210]    ● category;
[p210]    ● business unit;
[p210]    ● commercial significance;
[p210]    ● geographic or channel scope.
[p210] 
[p210] A user must not approve work exceeding their configured authority.
[p210] 
[p210]

[p212]      9.30.    GOV-030 AI Permission Inheritance
[p212] 
[p212] 
[p212] AI must operate using the permission context of the user invoking it.
[p212] 
[p213] AI must not access or reveal:
[p213] 
[p213]    ● resources outside the user's scope;
[p213]    ● restricted cost information;
[p213]    ● pricing initiatives the user cannot access;
[p213]    ● protected historical records;
[p213]    ● unavailable organisational information.
[p213]

[p213]     9.33.       GOV-033 User Suspension and Revocation
[p213] 
[p213] 
[p213] Suspending or deactivating a user must immediately prevent new protected actions.
[p213] 
[p213] Existing:
[p213] 
[p214]    ● comments;
[p214]    ● analyses;
[p214]    ● submissions;
[p214]    ● approvals;
[p214]    ● policy-authorisation records;
[p214]    ● execution records;
[p214]    ● audit events
[p214] 
[p214] must remain preserved.
[p214] 
[p214]

[p215]      9.36.      GOV-036 Governance Audit Trail
[p215] 
[p215] 
[p215] Consequential governance actions must be recorded with:
[p215] 
[p215]    ● actor;
[p215]    ● action;
[p215] 
[p216]    ● affected user or configuration;
[p216]    ● timestamp;
[p216]    ● previous state where relevant;
[p216]    ● resulting state.

[p218]     10.1.      AI-001 Contextual AI Assistance
[p218] 
[p218] 
[p218] AI assistance must be available within relevant pricing contexts, including:
[p218] 
[p218]    ● Pricing Initiatives;
[p218]    ● Pricing Builder;
[p218]    ● analysis and scenarios;
[p218]    ● proposals;
[p218]    ● review;
[p218]    ● collaboration;
[p218]    ● historical pricing work.
[p218] 
[p218] The AI should inherit the context of the object from which it is invoked.
[p218] 
[p218]

[p219]      10.3.     AI-003 Permission Inheritance
[p219] 
[p219] 
[p219] AI must operate within the permissions of the user invoking it.
[p219] 
[p219] The AI must not access, infer or expose information the user is not authorised to view.
[p219] 
[p219] This includes restrictions relating to:
[p219] 
[p219]     ● initiatives;
[p219]     ● organisational scope;
[p219]     ● cost information;
[p219]     ● sensitive financial information;
[p219]     ● historical pricing records;
[p219]     ● governance configuration.
[p219]

[p219]      10.5.     AI-005 Pricing Context Interpretation
[p219] 
[p219] 
[p219] Users must be able to ask AI to help interpret available pricing context.
[p219] 
[p219] AI may assist with:
[p219] 
[p219]     ● summarising the pricing situation;
[p219]     ● explaining relevant historical context;
[p219]     ● identifying important available inputs;
[p219]     ● highlighting missing information;
[p219]     ● identifying conflicting information;
[p219] 
[p220]    ● explaining the relationship between pricing inputs and the current initiative.
[p220]

[p220]     10.6.       AI-006 Pricing Work Construction
[p220] 
[p220] 
[p220] AI may assist users in developing pricing work.
[p220] 
[p220] This includes:
[p220] 
[p220]    ● drafting pricing strategies;
[p220]    ● converting business intent into structured pricing logic;
[p220]    ● drafting rules and conditions;
[p220]    ● suggesting parameters;
[p220]    ● identifying required assumptions;
[p220]    ● refining rationale;
[p220]    ● helping structure a Pricing Proposal.
[p220] 
[p220] AI-created material remains proposed content until explicitly applied by an authorised user.
[p220] 
[p220]

[p221]      10.8.     AI-008 Structured Output Validation
[p221] 
[p221] 
[p221] AI-generated structured pricing work must pass the same product validation applied to
[p221] human-created pricing work.
[p221] 
[p221] AI output must not bypass:
[p221] 
[p221]     ● required fields;
[p221]     ● formula validation;
[p221]     ● scope validation;
[p221]     ● guardrail evaluation;
[p221]     ● permissions;
[p221]     ● workflow rules.
[p221]

[p221]      10.9.     AI-009 Analysis Assistance
[p221] 
[p221] 
[p221] AI may assist users in interpreting pricing analysis.
[p221] 
[p221] It may:
[p221] 
[p221]     ● compare scenarios;
[p221]     ● explain financial implications;
[p221]     ● highlight material differences;
[p221]     ● identify unusual outcomes;
[p221]     ● surface trade-offs;
[p221]     ● identify entities requiring closer inspection;
[p221] 
[p222]    ● summarise guardrail results.
[p222]

[p223]    10.12.     AI-012 Risk and Inconsistency Detection
[p223] 
[p223] 
[p223] AI may identify potential issues including:
[p223] 
[p223]    ● contradictory assumptions;
[p223]    ● inconsistent rationale;
[p223]    ● missing supporting context;
[p223]    ● unusual pricing changes;
[p223]    ● incomplete proposal explanations;
[p223]    ● conflicts between stated objectives and proposed actions.
[p223] 
[p223] AI-generated risk identification must remain advisory unless the condition is independently
[p223] enforced by a product guardrail or validation rule.
[p223] 
[p223]

[p223]    10.14.     AI-014 Collaboration Assistance
[p223] 
[p223] 
[p223] AI may assist with collaborative pricing work by:
[p223] 
[p223]    ● summarising discussion;
[p223]    ● identifying unresolved questions;
[p223]    ● synthesising stakeholder input;
[p223]    ● identifying conflicting positions;
[p223]    ● summarising requested changes;
[p223]    ● preparing review summaries.
[p223] 
[p223] AI must not falsely attribute statements or decisions to users who did not make them.
[p223] 
[p223]

[p223]    10.15.     AI-015 Proposal Preparation
[p223] 
[p224] AI may assist users in preparing a Pricing Proposal by summarising:
[p224] 
[p224]    ● objective;
[p224]    ● proposed action;
[p224]    ● pricing scope;
[p224]    ● relevant economics;
[p224]    ● assumptions;
[p224]    ● guardrail status;
[p224]    ● supporting evidence;
[p224]    ● key risks;
[p224]    ● material changes.
[p224] 
[p224] The final submitted proposal must remain explicitly submitted by an authorised human user.
[p224] 
[p224]

[p225]    10.21.     AI-021 Uncertainty and Missing Basis
[p225] 
[p225] 
[p225] Where available information does not support a reliable conclusion, AI must indicate that
[p225] limitation rather than inventing missing pricing facts.
[p225] 
[p226] AI must not fabricate:
[p226] 
[p226]    ● competitor prices;
[p226]    ● costs;
[p226]    ● demand figures;
[p226]    ● margins;
[p226]    ● exchange rates;
[p226]    ● customer behaviour;
[p226]    ● approval authority;
[p226]    ● organisational policy.
[p226]

[p227]    10.27.      AI-027 AI and Guardrails
[p227] 
[p227] 
[p227] AI may assist users in understanding applicable organisational Guardrails.
[p227] 
[p227] Where permitted by the user's access, AI may explain or summarise:
[p227] 
[p227]    ● the Guardrail definition;
[p227]    ● applicable scope;
[p227]    ● configured threshold or condition;
[p227]    ● applicable configuration version;
[p227]    ● ANALYSIS-014 evaluation result;
[p227]    ● GOV-025 behavioural treatment;
[p227]    ● the effect of the Guardrail on Proposal or workflow progression.
[p227] 
[p227] AI must consume authoritative Guardrail information from the applicable product records.
[p227] 
[p227] AI must not independently:
[p227] 
[p227]    ● create an organisational Guardrail through ordinary pricing work;
[p227] 
[p228]    ● determine Guardrail applicability where governance has not established it;
[p228]    ● redefine a Guardrail;
[p228]    ● modify a Guardrail threshold, operator, scope or behavioural treatment;
[p228]    ● calculate a competing Guardrail result;
[p228]    ● replace an ANALYSIS-014 evaluation with AI reasoning;
[p228]    ● infer Near Threshold where the configured Guardrail does not define the required
[p228]        proximity rule;
[p228]    ● represent Unable to Evaluate as Within Guardrail;
[p228]    ● override a Guardrail;
[p228]    ● ignore a Blocking treatment;
[p228]    ● claim that an exception or authorised progression path exists where none has been
[p228]        configured.
[p228] 
[p228] AI may identify a possible need for a new or changed Guardrail as an advisory observation,
[p228] but that observation must not become organisational governance until an authorised user
[p228] completes the applicable governance action.
[p228] 
[p228] Where AI converts user pricing intent into structured Builder content, a user-defined pricing
[p228] boundary must remain an initiative-specific condition or constraint unless it references an
[p228] existing organisational Guardrail.
[p228] 
[p228] AI therefore assists with interpretation and explanation while Guardrail definition, evaluation
[p228] and behavioural consequence remain controlled by GOV-023, ANALYSIS-014 and GOV-025
[p228] respectively.
[p228] 
[p228]

[p229]    10.30.     AI-030 AI and Governance Administration
[p229] 
[p229] 
[p229] AI may explain existing workspace governance where the invoking user has access.
[p229] 
[p229] AI must not autonomously:
[p229] 
[p229]    ● grant permissions;
[p229]    ● assign commercial authority;
[p229]    ● modify approval limits;
[p229]    ● alter guardrails;
[p229]    ● activate policy autonomy;
[p229]    ● change workflow governance.
[p229]

[p235]     11.4.     EXEC-004 Execution Instructions
[p235] 
[p235] 
[p235] The authorised pricing action must provide enough structured information for the
[p235] implementation owner to determine exactly what must be implemented.
[p235] 
[p235] Where applicable, execution instructions should identify:
[p235] 
[p235]    ● affected priceable entity or pricing scope;
[p235]    ● applicable location, channel, customer group or other pricing dimension;
[p235]    ● relevant current or baseline pricing state;
[p235]    ● authorised price, pricing value, pricing rule or pricing logic;
[p235]    ● effective date or effective period;
[p235]    ● temporary pricing period where applicable;
[p235]    ● authorised exceptions;
[p235]    ● implementation conditions;
[p235]    ● relevant operational instructions.
[p235] 
[p235] Execution instructions must derive from the exact controlled Pricing Proposal version
[p235] providing execution authority.
[p235] 
[p235] The execution layer must not silently substitute later workspace values, another Scenario,
[p235] another Proposal version or changed pricing logic for the authorised execution baseline.
[p235] 
[p235]

[p240]    11.13.       EXEC-013 Execution History
[p240] 
[p240] 
[p240] Consequential execution events must be recorded in chronological history.
[p240] 
[p240] This includes:
[p240] 
[p240]    ● assignment;
[p240]    ● readiness for execution;
[p240]    ● implementation started;
[p240]    ● partial implementation;
[p240]    ● implementation confirmation;
[p240]    ● implementation failure;
[p240]    ● implementation variance;
[p240]    ● correction.
[p240]

[p242]    11.17.        EXEC-017 Monitoring Metrics
[p242] 
[p242] 
[p242] Users must be able to record relevant post-implementation outcomes where information is
[p242] available.
[p242] 
[p242] Supported outcome categories may include:
[p242] 
[p242]     ● sales;
[p242]     ● revenue;
[p242]     ● volume;
[p242]     ● gross margin;
[p242]     ● gross profit;
[p242]     ● customer response;
[p242] 
[p243]    ● commercial feedback;
[p243]    ● operational feedback;
[p243]    ● pricing exceptions.
[p243]

[p243]    11.20.     EXEC-020 Qualitative Outcome Recording
[p243] 
[p243] 
[p243] Monitoring must not require every pricing outcome to be expressed numerically.
[p243] 
[p243] Users must be able to record qualitative outcomes such as:
[p243] 
[p243]    ● customer resistance;
[p243]    ● improved competitiveness;
[p243]    ● distributor feedback;
[p243]    ● sales-team feedback;
[p243]    ● unexpected operational consequences;
[p243]    ● competitor response.
[p243]

[p245]    11.26.     EXEC-026 Completion Summary
[p245] 
[p245] 
[p245] A completed Pricing Initiative should preserve a concise final summary sufficient to connect
[p245] the original pricing objective with the organisational decision, actual implementation and
[p245] recorded outcome.
[p245] 
[p245] The summary should contain, where applicable:
[p245] 
[p245]    ● original pricing objective;
[p245]    ● exact Pricing Proposal version that provided execution authority;
[p245]    ● whether that Proposal version was Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
[p245]    ● authorised pricing action;
[p245]    ● actual implemented pricing action or state;
[p245]    ● significant execution variances or Implementation Exceptions;
[p245]    ● relevant monitored outcomes;
[p245]    ● final objective assessment;
[p245]    ● final Pricing Initiative status.
[p245] 
[p245] The completion summary must distinguish what was authorised from what was actually
[p245] implemented.
[p245] 
[p245] It must not overwrite or simplify away material differences preserved in the Proposal,
[p245] execution or monitoring records.
[p245]

[p246]    11.29.     EXEC-029 Execution Auditability
[p246] 
[p246] 
[p246] For every consequential implemented pricing action, Owómidé must preserve enough
[p246] information to determine:
[p246] 
[p246]     ● which exact Pricing Proposal version provided execution authority;
[p246]     ● whether that Proposal version was Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
[p246]     ● what exact pricing action and scope were authorised;
[p246]     ● who was responsible for implementation;
[p246]     ● what was actually implemented;
[p246]     ● when implementation occurred;
[p246]     ● whether implementation matched the authorised decision baseline;
[p246]     ● what execution variance or Implementation Exception occurred, where applicable;
[p246]     ● what happened after implementation.
[p246] 
[p246] Execution auditability must preserve the distinction between organisational decision authority
[p246] and actual implementation.
[p246] 
[p246] A later Pricing Proposal, pricing change or workspace update must not rewrite the execution
[p246] basis or actual implementation record of the earlier action.
[p246] 
[p246]

[p249]     12.1.     HIST-001 Persistent Pricing Record
[p249] 
[p249] 
[p249] Every Pricing Initiative must maintain a persistent historical record containing the relevant:
[p249] 
[p249]    ● objective;
[p249]    ● pricing scope;
[p249]    ● pricing context;
[p249]    ● assumptions;
[p249]    ● pricing logic;
[p249]    ● analysis;
[p249]    ● scenarios;
[p249]    ● proposals;
[p249]    ● collaboration;
[p249]    ● reviews;
[p249]    ● organisational decision records, including approvals and policy authorisations;
[p249]    ● execution;
[p249]    ● monitoring;
[p249]    ● lifecycle events.
[p249] 
[p249] Closing or completing an initiative must not remove this record.
[p249] 
[p249]

[p253]     12.9.     HIST-009 Activity Timeline
[p253] 
[p253] 
[p253] Every Pricing Initiative must contain a chronological activity timeline.
[p253] 
[p253] Relevant events should include:
[p253] 
[p253]    ● initiative creation;
[p253]    ● scope changes;
[p253]    ● significant edits;
[p253]    ● comments;
[p253]    ● assignments;
[p253]    ● evidence additions;
[p253]    ● scenario creation;
[p253]    ● review submission;
[p253]    ● proposal submission;
[p253]    ● requested changes;
[p253]    ● approvals;
[p253]    ● policy authorisations;
[p253]    ● rejections;
[p253]    ● escalation;
[p253] 
[p254]    ● execution events;
[p254]    ● monitoring updates;
[p254]    ● completion;
[p254]    ● cancellation.
[p254]

[p254]    12.12.      HIST-012 Administrative History
[p254] 
[p254] 
[p254] Consequential administrative changes affecting pricing governance must remain historically
[p254] available.
[p254] 
[p254] Examples include:
[p254] 
[p254]    ● role changes;
[p254]    ● permission changes;
[p254]    ● approval-authority changes;
[p254]    ● workflow changes;
[p254]    ● guardrail changes;
[p254] 
[p255]     ● cost-access changes;
[p255]     ● policy-authorisation configuration changes.
[p255]

[p255]    12.16.       HIST-016 Searchable Pricing History
[p255] 
[p255] 
[p255] Authorised users must be able to search historical pricing work.
[p255] 
[p255] Search should support relevant criteria such as:
[p255] 
[p255]     ● Pricing Initiative;
[p255]     ● product or SKU;
[p255]     ● category;
[p255]     ● owner;
[p255]     ● initiative type;
[p255]     ● status;
[p255]     ● decision date;
[p255]     ● implementation date;
[p255]     ● proposal outcome.
[p255] 
[p256] Search results must respect user permissions and scope.
[p256] 
[p256]

[p256]    12.17.     HIST-017 Historical Filtering
[p256] 
[p256] 
[p256] Users should be able to filter pricing history by relevant dimensions including:
[p256] 
[p256]    ● completed;
[p256]    ● rejected;
[p256]    ● cancelled;
[p256]    ● product or category;
[p256]    ● date range;
[p256]    ● location;
[p256]    ● channel;
[p256]    ● initiative type;
[p256]    ● responsible user.
[p256]

[p256]    12.18.     HIST-018 Historical Initiative View
[p256] 
[p256] 
[p256] A historical Pricing Initiative must remain understandable as a coherent record.
[p256] 
[p256] Users should be able to determine:
[p256] 
[p256]    ● why the initiative existed;
[p256]    ● what was considered;
[p256]    ● what was proposed;
[p256]    ● what changed;
[p256]    ● what organisational decision was made, including whether the applicable Pricing
[p256]        Proposal was Approved or Authorised Within Policy;
[p256]    ● what was implemented;
[p256]    ● what outcome was recorded.
[p256]

[p256]    12.19.     HIST-019 Organisational Pricing Knowledge
[p256] 
[p256] 
[p256] Completed pricing work should become reusable organisational knowledge.
[p256] 
[p256] Relevant previous work may provide context for:
[p256] 
[p256]    ● future Pricing Initiatives;
[p256]    ● pricing strategies;
[p256]    ● assumptions;
[p256] 
[p257]     ● scenarios;
[p257]     ● decision rationale;
[p257]     ● recurring pricing situations.
[p257]

[p257]    12.22.     HIST-022 Decision Rationale Preservation
[p257] 
[p257] 
[p257] The system must preserve enough structured and contextual information to explain why a
[p257] consequential pricing decision was made.
[p257] 
[p257] Rationale may include:
[p257] 
[p257]     ● stated objective;
[p257]     ● assumptions;
[p257] 
[p258]        ● supporting evidence;
[p258]        ● analysis;
[p258]        ● stakeholder input;
[p258]        ● proposal explanation;
[p258]        ● Approval comments or Policy Authorisation evaluation information.
[p258] 
[p258] A final price alone is not a sufficient historical record.
[p258] 
[p258]

[p258]    12.25.         HIST-025 Audit Search
[p258] 
[p258] 
[p258] Users with appropriate audit authority should be able to inspect activity by relevant
[p258] dimensions such as:
[p258] 
[p258]        ● user;
[p258]        ● action type;
[p258]        ● Pricing Initiative;
[p258]        ● proposal;
[p258]        ● date range.
[p258]

[p262]      13.1.    NOTIF-001 Personal Work Queue
[p262] 
[p262] 
[p262] Every active user should have a personal work view containing pricing work that currently
[p262] requires or may require their attention.
[p262] 
[p262] This may include initiatives where the user is:
[p262] 
[p262]    ● owner;
[p262]    ● collaborator;
[p262]    ● reviewer;
[p262]    ● approver;
[p262]    ● assignee;
[p262]    ● implementation owner;
[p262]    ● mentioned;
[p262]    ● responsible for monitoring activity.
[p262]

[p262]      13.2.    NOTIF-002 Action Required Separation
[p262] 
[p262] 
[p262] The system must distinguish between information that is merely visible to a user and work
[p262] that requires an explicit action from that user.
[p262] 
[p262] Examples of action-required items include:
[p262] 
[p262]    ● assignment due;
[p262]    ● review requested;
[p262]    ● approval requested;
[p262]    ● requested clarification;
[p262]    ● execution assigned;
[p262]    ● monitoring input required.
[p262]

[p263]      13.3.     NOTIF-003 Workspace-Level Visibility
[p263] 
[p263] 
[p263] Authorised users must be able to view relevant organisation-level pricing activity.
[p263] 
[p263] The workspace should provide visibility into categories such as:
[p263] 
[p263]    ● active Pricing Initiatives;
[p263]    ● initiatives requiring attention;
[p263]    ● pending reviews;
[p263]    ● pending approvals;
[p263]    ● ready-for-execution work;
[p263]    ● implementation in progress;
[p263]    ● monitoring activity;
[p263]    ● recently completed initiatives.
[p263] 
[p263] Visibility must respect the user's role and scope.
[p263] 
[p263]

[p263]      13.4.     NOTIF-004 Pricing Initiative Status Visibility
[p263] 
[p263] 
[p263] Pricing Initiative listings must display enough information for users to understand current
[p263] state without opening every initiative.
[p263] 
[p263] Relevant information should include:
[p263] 
[p263]    ● initiative title;
[p263]    ● initiative type;
[p263]    ● owner;
[p263]    ● pricing scope summary;
[p263]    ● lifecycle state;
[p263]    ● urgency;
[p263]    ● current responsible party;
[p263]    ● next required action;
[p263]    ● target or effective date where applicable.
[p263]

[p263]      13.5.     NOTIF-005 Notification Events
[p263] 
[p263] 
[p263] The MVP must generate notifications for consequential events where user awareness or
[p263] action is required.
[p263] 
[p263] These should include, where relevant:
[p263] 
[p264]    ● user assignment;
[p264]    ● mention;
[p264]    ● review request;
[p264]    ● approval request;
[p264]    ● changes requested;
[p264]    ● proposal rejection;
[p264]    ● proposal approval;
[p264]    ● proposal policy authorisation;
[p264]    ● escalation;
[p264]    ● upcoming due date;
[p264]    ● overdue action;
[p264]    ● execution assignment;
[p264]    ● implementation issue;
[p264]    ● monitoring assignment.
[p264]

[p264]     13.6.     NOTIF-006 Notification Recipient
[p264] 
[p264] 
[p264] Notifications must be sent only to users relevant to the event.
[p264] 
[p264] The system must determine recipients based on:
[p264] 
[p264]    ● assignment;
[p264]    ● workflow responsibility;
[p264]    ● role;
[p264]    ● scope;
[p264]    ● mention;
[p264]    ● ownership;
[p264]    ● organisational configuration.
[p264] 
[p264] A notification must never provide an unauthorised user access to protected pricing
[p264] information.
[p264] 
[p264]

[p266]    13.12.      NOTIF-012 Pending Approval Queue
[p266] 
[p266] 
[p266] Users with approval authority must have a clear view of proposals currently awaiting their
[p266] decision.
[p266] 
[p266] Each approval item should show relevant information such as:
[p266] 
[p266]    ● Pricing Initiative;
[p266]    ● proposal version;
[p266]    ● submitting user;
[p266]    ● submitted date;
[p266]    ● urgency;
[p266]    ● pricing scope;
[p266]    ● current workflow stage;
[p266]    ● approval deadline where applicable.
[p266]

[p266]    13.13.      NOTIF-013 Review Queue
[p266] 
[p266] 
[p266] Users responsible for reviews must be able to identify:
[p266] 
[p266]    ● review requests assigned to them;
[p266]    ● review status;
[p266]    ● due date where applicable;
[p266]    ● initiative urgency.
[p266]

[p266]    13.14.      NOTIF-014 Execution Queue
[p266] 
[p266] 
[p266] Authorised Executors must be able to identify pricing actions that are:
[p266] 
[p266]    ● Ready for Execution;
[p266] 
[p267]    ● assigned to them;
[p267]    ● partially implemented;
[p267]    ● blocked;
[p267]    ● overdue.
[p267]

[p267]    13.18.      NOTIF-018 Escalation Visibility
[p267] 
[p267] 
[p267] Where an escalation occurs, relevant users must be able to identify:
[p267] 
[p267]    ● what was escalated;
[p267]    ● why escalation occurred;
[p267] 
[p268]    ● previous responsible party;
[p268]    ● current responsible party;
[p268]    ● resulting action required.

[p269]    13.24.      NOTIF-024 Workspace Filtering
[p269] 
[p269] 
[p269] Workspace-level work views should support filtering by relevant dimensions such as:
[p269] 
[p269]    ● status;
[p269]    ● owner;
[p269]    ● initiative type;
[p269]    ● urgency;
[p269]    ● category;
[p269]    ● product;
[p269]    ● location;
[p269]    ● channel;
[p269]    ● responsible participant;
[p269]    ● date range.
[p269] 
[p269] Filtering must respect user access scope.
[p269] 
[p269]

[p269]    13.25.      NOTIF-025 Workspace Search
[p269] 
[p269] 
[p269] Users must be able to search pricing work they are authorised to access.
[p269] 
[p269] Search should support relevant identifiers such as:
[p269] 
[p269]    ● Pricing Initiative title;
[p269]    ● product or SKU;
[p269]    ● category;
[p269]    ● proposal identifier;
[p269]    ● owner.
[p269]

[p274]      14.3.     EXPORT-003 Execution Export Content
[p274] 
[p274] 
[p274] Where applicable, an execution-oriented export should include:
[p274] 
[p274]     ● Pricing Initiative identifier;
[p274]     ● proposal identifier and version;
[p274]     ● Priceable Entity identifier;
[p274]     ● Priceable Entity name;
[p274]     ● Priceable Entity type;
[p274]     ● applicable category;
[p274]     ● location;
[p274]     ● channel;
[p274]     ● other relevant pricing dimensions;
[p274]     ● Current Price or other pricing baseline preserved in the exact authorised Proposal
[p274]         version's decision snapshot, where applicable and permitted;
[p274]     ● authorised price, pricing value, pricing rule or pricing logic, as applicable;
[p274]     ● currency;
[p274]     ● effective date;
[p274]     ● authorised exceptions;
[p274]     ● implementation notes where applicable.
[p274] 
[p274] The output must contain enough information for the receiving operational team to identify
[p274] what should change.
[p274] 
[p274] For execution-oriented exports affecting multiple Priceable Entities, each exported pricing
[p274] instruction must remain attributable to the applicable Priceable Entity and relevant pricing
[p274] dimensions.
[p274] 
[p274] A category, product group or other grouping selector must not replace the Priceable Entity
[p274] identifier required for operational implementation unless the receiving operational system
[p274] explicitly implements pricing at that same directly priceable object level.
[p274] 
[p274]

[p275]     14.6.     EXPORT-006 Export Permission
[p275] 
[p275] 
[p275] Only users with appropriate export permissions may export protected pricing information.
[p275] 
[p275] Export authority must respect:
[p275] 
[p275]    ● organisation membership;
[p275]    ● role;
[p275]    ● resource;
[p275]    ● pricing scope;
[p275]    ● sensitive-data permissions.
[p275]

[p278]    14.13.     EXPORT-013 Export Attribution
[p278] 
[p278] 
[p278] Consequential exports must be attributable to the user who generated them.
[p278] 
[p278] The system must preserve:
[p278] 
[p278]    ● exporting user;
[p278]    ● timestamp;
[p278]    ● export type;
[p278]    ● related Pricing Initiative;
[p278]    ● proposal version where applicable.
[p278]

[p281]    14.26.     EXPORT-026 Export Validation
[p281] 
[p281] 
[p281] Before generating an execution-oriented export, the system must validate that:
[p281] 
[p281]    ● the proposal has valid execution authority;
[p281]    ● the proposal version is current and identifiable;
[p281]    ● required implementation fields are present;
[p281]    ● affected scope is defined;
[p281]    ● applicable effective dates are valid;
[p281]    ● no blocking execution condition remains.
[p281]
